course: biology
lesson: 286
title: From field to plate: wheat, flour and bread
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The slice of toast on your plate started as a grass. Wheat is a tall grass whose seeds, the grains, are ground into flour.
explain: A farmer sows the grain, the plants grow all spring, and in summer a combine harvester cuts the stalks, beats out the grains and blows the straw away in one go.
explain: The grain goes to a mill to become flour, the baker mixes the flour with water and yeast, and tiny living yeast cells breathe out gas that puffs the dough up. Baking sets the bubbles, and that is why bread has holes.
explain: Every step depends on soil, weather and peace: a wet harvest, a drought or a war that blocks the ships can change the price of bread on the other side of the world.
fact: Bread comes from wheat, a grass whose seeds are ground into flour; wheat was first farmed about 10,000 years ago in the Middle East.
fact: A combine harvester cuts the wheat, beats out the grains and blows away the straw at once, doing work that once took dozens of people with sickles.
fact: Windmills and watermills once ground grain between big stones; today most flour is made in huge roller mills.
fact: White flour is made from the inside of the grain, while wholemeal flour uses the whole grain, so it has more fibre.
fact: Yeast is a tiny fungus that eats sugar and makes bubbles of gas; stretchy gluten traps the gas so the dough rises, and baking kills the yeast and sets the holes.
fact: Ukraine has very rich black soil called chernozem and grows huge amounts of wheat and sunflowers, so it is called a breadbasket of the world.
fact: When the war blocked ships from Ukraine in 2022, grain and bread prices rose in many countries.
fact: Ploughing turns the soil and buries weeds, but many farmers now plough less to keep the soil, its worms and its microbes from drying and blowing away.
fact: Mice and rats eat stored grain, which is one reason farmers have kept cats for thousands of years.
fact: Rice, wheat and maize together give people about half of all their food energy; pasta is made from wheat, and tortillas are usually made from maize.
fact: Rapeseed and sunflowers make seeds that are pressed into cooking oil, and the yellow rapeseed fields are busy with bees in spring.
fact: Rain at harvest can spoil grain, frost can kill young plants and a drought can ruin a whole year's work, so farmers watch the weather closely.
fact: In the 1840s a plant disease destroyed Ireland's potatoes, the main food of the poor; about a million people died and about a million more left the country.
fact: The Svalbard seed vault in Norway keeps over a million seed samples of the world's crops inside a frozen mountain as a backup against war and disaster, because the cold keeps seeds alive for a very long time.
exercise: What is wheat? | A grass whose seeds are ground into flour | A tree whose fruit is dried and ground into flour | A root dug up and crushed into flour | A bean that grows in pods
exercise: What does a combine harvester do? | Cuts the wheat, beats out the grain and blows the straw away | Only waters the field | Plants the seeds in rows and covers them over with fresh soil | Bakes the bread on the farm
exercise: What did old windmills do with grain? | Ground it between big stones into flour | Pumped it out to sea along canals | Dried it into straw | Turned it into yeast for bakers
exercise: Why is wholemeal flour better for you than white flour? | It uses the whole grain, so it has more fibre | It is made from a different plant, which is healthier | It has extra sugar added | It has no grain in it at all
exercise: What makes bread dough rise? | Yeast, a tiny fungus, makes bubbles of gas | Flour swells up when it gets warm in the oven | Salt explodes in the oven | Water turns to ice inside it
exercise: Why are there holes in a slice of bread? | Gas bubbles from yeast were set by baking | The baker pokes them in with a fork before baking | Hungry mice nibble them in the bakery at night | The flour already had tiny holes in every grain
exercise: Why is Ukraine called a breadbasket? | Its rich black soil grows huge amounts of wheat | It makes most of the world's baskets | Bread was invented there just last century | It has to buy in all its bread from abroad
exercise: Why do many farmers now plough less? | To keep the soil and its living things from drying and blowing away | Because tractors are against the law | Because wheat grows only on hard ground | Because ploughing makes the grain taste sweeter, and people want plain bread
exercise: Why have farmers kept cats for thousands of years? | They catch mice and rats that eat stored grain | They help pull the plough | They scare the birds off the young wheat in spring | They keep the hens warm
exercise: Which three grains feed most of the world? | Rice, wheat and maize | Oats, rye and pearl barley | Rice, beans and potatoes | Wheat, sugar beet and peas
exercise: What are bright yellow rapeseed fields grown for? | Their seeds give cooking oil | Their flowers are made into yellow paint | Their stems are made into paper | Their leaves are fed to bees in winter
exercise: Why does a farmer check the weather so often? | Rain at harvest, frost or drought can spoil the crop | Wheat grows only on cloudy days | Tractors cannot start in the sun | Farmers must report it to the council every single morning
exercise: What happened in the Irish potato famine? | A disease destroyed the potatoes and about a million people died | Too many potatoes made people ill | The potatoes were all sold to Spain | A great flood washed away all the wheat and the barley
exercise: What is the Svalbard seed vault for? | Keeping seeds of the world's crops safe as a backup | Selling packets of seeds to gardeners all over the world | Growing wheat under the snow | Storing bread for winter
exercise: Where was wheat first farmed? | In the Middle East about 10,000 years ago | In South America about 500 years ago, by the Incas | In Ukraine about 200 years ago, on the steppe | In Australia about 50 years ago, on big farms
test: «why's bread got holes, did the baker mess up» What is right? | No, gas bubbles from the yeast made them and baking set them | Yes, good bread has no holes | The holes are where the seeds fell out when the loaf was sliced | Mice made them at night
test: «war's miles away, why's my bread dearer» How can that be? | The war blocked ships carrying wheat, so there was less grain to sell | Bakers just wanted more money for no reason at all, and used the war as an excuse | Bread is made in the war zone | Wheat stops growing during any war
test: Count them: a combine has 4 wheels and pulls a trailer with 2 wheels; 3 such teams work the field. How many wheels? | 18 | 12 | 6 | 24
test: What would happen if a baker forgot the yeast? | The bread would come out flat and heavy | The bread would rise even higher than usual | The bread would turn into a sweet sponge cake | The bread would taste much too strongly of sugar
test: «only one kind of potato, cheap and easy, what's the problem» What did Ireland in the 1840s teach? | One disease can wipe out a crop everyone depends on | One kind of potato never gets ill, so it is the safest | Potatoes grow better without variety | Famines happen only with wheat
test: In a cartoon a farmer pulls bread loaves straight off a wheat plant. What is real? | Grain must be harvested, ground into flour, mixed with yeast and baked | Bread really grows on wheat, but only on the very tallest plants in the field | Wheat makes loaves after rain | Only white bread grows that way
test: Why does a seed vault sit inside a frozen mountain? | Cold keeps seeds alive for a very long time | Seeds grow best in ice | Mountains make the seeds bigger | The cold keeps birds and mice away from the seeds
test: «we had rice for dinner, my mate had pasta, his gran had tortillas. who ate a big grain» | All three: rice, wheat and maize | Only the rice eater | Only the pasta eater | None of them
test: «field's gone bright yellow, is it mustard for hotdogs» What is it more likely to be? | Rapeseed, grown for cooking oil | Sunflowers before they open | Wheat that has gone bad | Paint sprayed by the farmer
test: What would happen to a barn of grain with no cats and no traps? | Mice and rats would eat and spoil much of it | The grain would last longer | The grain would sprout and grow into tall wheat | Birds would guard it
test: Why might a field left bare and ploughed in a dry windy spring lose its soil? | Wind blows the loose dry soil away | Worms carry it off deep underground | Bare soil slowly turns into hard stone | Rain makes the soil float up and away
exam: Identify the agent responsible for the rising of bread dough. | Yeast, a microscopic fungus that liberates gas upon consuming sugar | The expansion of flour upon contact with the heat of the oven | A chemical reaction of salt with water | Air introduced by the windmill during the grinding of the grain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of Ukraine in respect of the world's grain supply? | Its fertile black soil yields large quantities of wheat for export | It imports the greater part of its grain from abroad | It produces chiefly rice in flooded fields | Its soil is unsuitable for cereal cultivation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Irish famine of the 1840s. | A disease of the potato, the staple food of the poor, resulted in approximately one million deaths | An excess of wheat caused widespread illness | The famine was occasioned by a shortage of rice | No loss of life was recorded
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the seed vault situated upon Svalbard. | The preservation of crop seeds as a safeguard against war and disaster | The commercial sale of seeds to gardeners | The cultivation of wheat beneath the ice | The storage of flour for the population of Norway in time of war
